) (slope 1). So L2 covers (1,2),(2,3).\n\n- L3: passes through (1,3) and (3,1) maybe? Let's find line through (1,3) and (3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't use that. Maybe (1,3) and (2,1): slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L3: (1,3)-(2,1). That covers (1,3),(2,1). Also passes through (3,-1) not needed.\n\n- L4: passes through (1,4) and (4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So can't use that. Maybe (1,4) and (3,2): slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. (1,4) and (2,2): slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L4: (1,4)-(2,2). But (2,2) already covered by L1. Overlap is okay but we need to cover (3,1) and (4,1). L4 passes through (3,0) not needed. So we still need to cover (3,1) and (4,1). They are not covered yet. So we need to adjust.\n\nMaybe we can assign L1 to cover (1,1) and (3,1) (vertical? No, slope 0? Actually line through (1,1) and (3,1) is horizontal (slope 0), not allowed. So can't.\n\nMaybe we can use a line that passes through (1,4) and (3,1) with slope -1. Not allowed. So (1,4) cannot be paired with any point in column 3 or 4 except maybe (2,? ) with slope not -1. Let's try (1,4) and (2,3): slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. (1,4) and (2,2): slope -2, allowed. So L4: (1,4)-(2,2). Already considered. That covers (1,4),(2,2). But (2,2) already covered by L1. Overlap okay.\n\nNow we need to cover (3,1) and (4,1). Could we have a line that passes through (2,1) and (3,1)? That's horizontal (slope 0), not allowed. So we need a line that passes through (3,1) and some column 1 point maybe? But column 1 points are already used. However, we could have a line that passes through (3,1) and (1,? ) with slope not -1. Let's try (3,1) and (1,2): slope = (2-1)/(1-3) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, allowed. So line through (1,2) and (3,1) is allowed. But (1,2) is already used by L2. Overlap okay. So we could have L2 also cover (3,1). But L2 currently passes through (1,2) and (2,3). Does line through (1,2) and (2,3) also pass through (3,1)? Let's check: line through (1,2) and (2,3) has equation y-2 = (3-2)/(2-1)*(x-1) => y-2 = 1*(x-1) => y = x+1. At x=3, y=4, not 1. So L2 does not pass through (3,1). So we need a line that passes through (3,1) and some column 1 point. Let's try (1,1) and (3,1): horizontal, not allowed. (1,2) and (3,1): slope -0.5, allowed. So we could define a line L5: (1,2)-(3,1). But we only have 4 lines total. So we need to reassign lines.\n\nMaybe we can use a line that passes through (1,2) and (3,1) and also (2,? )? Let's see line through (1,2) and (3,1) has slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5. Equation: y-2 = -0.5*(x-1) => y = -0.5x + 2.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 2.5 = 1.5, not integer. So it doesn't pass through any integer point in column 2. So that line would cover (1,2) and (3,1) but not any column 2 point. That's okay, we can have a line that covers only column 1 and column 3 points. So we could have L2 be that line, covering (1,2) and (3,1). Then we need to cover (2,3) and (2,2) and (2,1) and (4,1). Let's try to assign.\n\nWe have 4 lines: each must pass through a distinct column 1 point. Let's assign:\n\n- L1: (1,1) with something else.\n- L2: (1,2) with (3,1) (slope -0.5).\n- L3: (1,3) with something else.\n- L4: (1,4) with something else.\n\nNow we need to cover all points in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). We have 4 lines, each can intersect column 2 at most one point. So we need to assign each of these points to some line. Let's see which lines can pass through them.\n\n- L1: we can choose L1 to pass through (2,1) maybe? Let's try line through (1,1) and (2,1): horizontal, not allowed. So can't. L1 could pass through (2,2): line through (1,1) and (2,2) slope 1, allowed. So L1 can cover (2,2). L1 could also pass through (2,3): line through (1,1) and (2,3) slope = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. So L1 could cover (2,3) if we want. But we need to assign each column 2 point to a distinct line (or maybe same line can cover multiple column 2 points? No, each line can intersect column 2 at only one point, so each column 2 point must be assigned to a different line). So we need to assign (2,1) to some line, (2,2) to another, (2,3) to another. L2 currently passes through (1,2) and (3,1). It does not intersect column 2 at integer point. So L2 cannot cover any column 2 point. So we need L3 and L4 to cover the remaining column 2 points.\n\n- L3: passes through (1,3). Could pass through (2,1) maybe? Let's see line through (1,3) and (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L3 can cover (2,1). Good.\n\n- L4: passes through (1,4). Could pass through (2,3) maybe? Slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. Could pass through (2,2): slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L4 can cover (2,2). But (2,2) already covered by L1 maybe. Let's assign systematically.\n\nWe have 4 lines: L1, L2, L3, L4.\n\nGoal: cover all points:\n\nColumn 1: each line covers its designated column 1 point.\n\nColumn 2: need to cover (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). We have 4 lines, each can cover at most one column 2 point. So we need to assign each of these three points to three distinct lines. L2 cannot cover any column 2 point (since its line doesn't intersect column 2 at integer point). So we need to assign (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) to L1, L3, L4.\n\n- L1: (1,1) can be paired with (2,2) (slope 1) or (2,3) (slope 2). Choose one.\n\n- L3: (1,3) can be paired with (2,1) (slope -2) or (2,2) (slope -1, not allowed) or (2,3) (slope 0, not allowed). So L3 can only pair with (2,1). So assign L3 to (2,1).\n\n- L4: (1,4) can be paired with (2,2) (slope -2) or (2,3) (slope -1, not allowed) or (2,1) (slope -3, allowed). But (2,1) already assigned to L3. So L4 can pair with (2,2) or (2,1). Let's assign L4 to (2,2).\n\nNow L1 must cover (2,3) (since (2,2) assigned to L4). So L1: (1,1)-(2,3) slope 2, allowed.\n\nNow column 3: points (3,1),(3,2). We need to cover them. L2 currently passes through (3,1). Good. L1 passes through (1,1)-(2,3) line equation: y-1 = 2*(x-1) => y = 2x-1. At x=3, y=5, not needed. So L1 does not cover column 3 points. L3 passes through (1,3)-(2,1) line equation: slope -2, y-3 = -2*(x-1) => y = -2x+5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1, not needed. So L3 does not cover column 3 points. L4 passes through (1,4)-(2,2) line: slope -2, y-4 = -2*(x-1) => y = -2x+6. At x=3, y = -6+6 = 0, not needed. So L4 does not cover column 3 points. So only L2 covers (3,1). We still need to cover (3,2). We have no line left to cover it. Could we adjust L2 to also cover (3,2)? L2 currently passes through (1,2)-(3,1). Let's see if line through (1,2) and (3,2) is slope = (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, not allowed. So can't. Could we assign L1 to cover (3,2)? L1 is line through (1,1)-(2,3) slope 2. At x=3, y = 2*3-1 = 5, not 2. So no. L3: line through (1,3)-(2,1) slope -2, at x=3, y = -2*3+5 = -1, not 2. L4: line through (1,4)-(2,2) slope -2, at x=3, y = -2*3+6 = 0, not 2. So none cover (3,2). So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nMaybe we can assign L2 to cover (3,2) instead of (3,1). Let's try line through (1,2) and (3,2): slope 0, not allowed. So can't. Maybe we can assign L2 to cover (3,2) and some other column 1 point? But L2 must pass through a column 1 point. Could be (1,3) maybe? Let's try line through (1,3) and (3,2): slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed. So we could have L2 be (1,3)-(3,2). But then L2 would not cover (1,2) column 1 point. So we need to reassign column 1 points accordingly.\n\nWe need to cover all column 1 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). So each line must pass through one of these. So we can assign L2 to (1,3) instead of (1,2). Then we need another line to cover (1,2). So we need to reassign.\n\nLet's try a systematic approach: we need to find 4 lines that cover all points. This is a combinatorial geometry problem. It might be possible, but we need to check if k=4 is possible for n=4. If it is, then the answer {0,1,2,3} would be wrong because k=4 is possible. But we need to verify if k=4 is indeed possible or not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is indeed {0,1,2,3} and k≥4 impossible for all n≥3. Let's try to prove impossibility for k≥4. The solution says \"Impossible by column counting\". Let's examine that argument.\n\nWe have n columns, column i has n+1-i points. If we have k sunny lines, each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines (since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once). The rest must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Among those, vertical lines can cover all points in a column. Horizontal lines can cover all points in a row. Slope -1 lines can cover at most one point per column as well (since they intersect each column at exactly one point). So only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column. So to cover column i with n+1-i points, we need at least (n+1-i - k) vertical lines? Actually each vertical line can cover all points in a column, but we cannot have multiple vertical lines covering the same column (they would be the same line). So we need to assign vertical lines to columns where the number of points not covered by sunny lines is >0. But we have only v = n - k vertical lines. So we can assign at most v columns to be covered by vertical lines. The rest columns must be covered by sunny lines (or horizontal lines). But horizontal lines cannot cover multiple points in a column, they can cover at most one point per column (since they intersect each column at exactly one point). So horizontal lines are not helpful for covering many points in a column. So we need vertical lines for columns with many points.\n\nThus we need to choose v columns to be covered by vertical lines. The remaining columns must be covered by sunny lines (or slope -1 lines). But each column not covered by a vertical line can have at most k points covered by sunny lines (since each sunny line can intersect that column at most once). So we need that for each column i not assigned a vertical line, the number of points in that column (n+1-i) ≤ k. So we need to choose v columns such that for all other columns, n+1-i ≤ k. That is, columns with more than k points must be assigned vertical lines. So we need to assign vertical lines to all columns i where n+1-i > k, i.e., i n+1-k. So the number of such columns is (n - k). Indeed, columns i = 1,2,..., n-k have n+1-i > k. Let's check: For i = n-k, n+1-(n-k) = k+1 > k. So column n-k has k+1 points, which is >k. So we need vertical lines for columns i = 1,..., n-k. That's exactly n-k columns. So we need at least n-k vertical lines. Since we have exactly n-k vertical lines (if we have k sunny lines), we can assign each vertical line to one of those columns. So it's possible in principle.\n\nThus the column counting argument does not rule out k≥4; it just says we need vertical lines for columns with >k points. For k≥4, the columns with >k points are those with i ≤ n-k. For n large enough, n-k may be less than n, but we have exactly n-k vertical lines, so we can assign them. So column counting alone does not give impossibility for k≥4. However, there might be additional constraints: the vertical lines must be distinct, and we have exactly n-k vertical lines, which matches the number of columns that need vertical lines. So it's possible to assign vertical lines to those columns. Then the remaining columns (i > n-k) have at most k points each, which can be covered by the k sunny lines, each covering at most one point per column. So we need to check if we can assign the k sunny lines to cover all points in those columns. This is like a bipartite matching problem: we have columns i = n-k+1,..., n, each with at most k points. There are k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column, but can cover points across multiple columns. So we need to assign each point in those columns to a sunny line such that each sunny line can have at most one point per column. This is essentially a problem of covering a set of points in a grid with k lines of arbitrary slopes (not parallel to axes or slope -1). Is it always possible for any k? Not necessarily. For k=4, we need to cover columns i = n-3,..., n. Let's examine for n=5, k=4. Then columns with >4 points are i ≤ n-k = 1. So column 1 has 5 points, need a vertical line. So we have v = n-k = 1 vertical line, covering column 1. The remaining columns are 2,3,4,5. Column 2 has 4 points, column 3 has 3 points, column 4 has 2 points, column 5 has 1 point. So total points left = 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. We have k=4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most one point, so each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So total capacity = 4*4 = 16 points, enough. But we need to assign points to lines such that each line's points are collinear (i.e., lie on a single line). That's the main difficulty.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: given a set of points in columns 2..n, can we partition them into k lines (with slopes not 0, infinite, -1) such that each line contains at most one point per column? This is reminiscent of constructing a Latin rectangle or something. For k small, maybe it's possible; for k large, maybe not. The solution claims k≥4 impossible. But we need to verify.\n\nLet's try to find a counterexample: For n=5, k=4, can we find 4 sunny lines covering all points in columns 2..5? Let's try to construct.\n\nPoints:\n\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4) (since a+b ≤ 6 => b ≤ 6-2 = 4). So points (2,1)-(2,4).\n\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) (b ≤ 6-3 = 3).\n\nColumn 4: (4,1),(4,2) (b ≤ 6-4 = 2).\n\nColumn 5: (5,1) (b ≤ 6-5 = 1).\n\nTotal points = 4+3+2+1 = 10.\n\nWe need 4 lines, each line can have at most one point per column. So each line can have up to 4 points (one from each column). So we need to partition the 10 points into 4 lines, each line having at most 4 points, and each line's points must be collinear with slope not 0, infinite, -1.\n\nWe can try to assign each line to cover a \"diagonal\" pattern: e.g., line with slope 1 passing through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3?) but (4,3) not in region (b=3 >2). So can't. Slope 1 line through (2,2),(3,3) works, but (4,4) not in region. So line can cover at most 2 points.\n\nMaybe we can use lines with slope 2: line through (2,1) and (3,3) slope = (3-1)/(3-2) = 2, allowed. That line also passes through (4,5) not needed. So line L1: (2,1),(3,3). It also could pass through (5,7) not needed. So L1 covers 2 points.\n\nLine with slope 0.5: line through (2,4) and (4,5) not needed. Actually slope 0.5 line through (2,4) and (4,5) passes through (3,4.5) not integer. So not helpful.\n\nWe need to cover all points. Let's try to see if we can assign each column's points to lines such that each line is collinear.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each can have at most 4 points. So we need to assign 10 points across 4 lines, average 2.5 points per line. So some lines will have 2 points, some 3 points.\n\nWe need to find 4 lines that cover all points. This is like a covering problem. Let's try to see if it's possible.\n\nWe can try to use vertical line for column 2? No, vertical lines are not sunny. So we cannot use vertical lines for column 2. So we need to use sunny lines for all points in column 2. That means each point in column 2 must be assigned to a different sunny line? Actually each sunny line can intersect column 2 at at most one point, so we have at most 4 points in column 2 that can be covered by 4 sunny lines. Column 2 has 4 points, so we need each sunny line to cover exactly one point in column 2. So each line must pass through a distinct point in column 2. So we need to assign each line to a distinct point in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). So each line will be determined by that point and some other points in other columns.\n\nNow we need to cover points in column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). Each line can intersect column 3 at at most one point. So we need to assign each of these three points to three of the four lines (one line will not intersect column 3). Similarly column 4: (4,1),(4,2) must be assigned to two lines. Column 5: (5,1) assigned to one line.\n\nThus we need to assign each line a set of points: each line has exactly one point from column 2 (by necessity), and possibly a point from column 3, column 4, column 5. So each line will have a pattern like (2,b2), (3,b3?), (4,b4?), (5,b5?) where each b_i is the y-coordinate for that column. The line must be straight, so the points must be collinear.\n\nThus we need to find a set of 4 lines each passing through a distinct point in column 2, and covering all points in columns 3-5.\n\nLet's denote the four lines L_a for a=1..4, each passes through (2, a) maybe? Actually column 2 points are (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Let's assign L1 passes through (2,1), L2 through (2,2), L3 through (2,3), L4 through (2,4). Now we need to assign points in column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We need to assign each to a line such that the line passes through both (2,b2) and (3,b3). That determines the line's slope. Then we need to see if that line can also pass through a point in column 4 (if needed) and column 5.\n\nLet's try to assign (3,1) to L1 (since L1 has (2,1)). Then L1 passes through (2,1) and (3,1). That's a horizontal line (slope 0), not allowed. So cannot assign (3,1) to L1. So (3,1) must be assigned to a line with a different column 2 point. Let's try assign (3,1) to L2 (2,2). The line through (2,2) and (3,1) has slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed (slope -1). So cannot assign (3,1) to L2. Assign (3,1) to L3 (2,3): slope (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed. So L3 can be line through (2,3) and (3,1). That line equation: slope -2, passes through (2,3): y-3 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x+7. At x=4, y = -8+7 = -1 (not needed). At x=5, y = -10+7 = -3 (not needed). So L3 covers (2,3) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow assign (3,2) to some line. It cannot be L1 (2,1) because slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. So L1 could be line through (2,1) and (3,2) slope 1. That line equation: y-1 = 1*(x-2) => y = x-1. At x=4, y=3 (but column 4 has points (4,1),(4,2) only; (4,3) not in region). So L1 would not intersect column 4 region. At x=5, y=4 (not needed). So L1 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nNow assign (3,3) to some line. Options: L2 (2,2) slope (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. So L2 could be line through (2,2) and (3,3) slope 1. Equation: y-2 = 1*(x-2) => y = x. At x=4, y=4 (not in region). At x=5, y=5 (not needed). So L2 covers (2,2) and (3,3). Good.\n\nNow we have assigned all column 3 points. So far we have:\n\n- L1: (2,1),(3,2)\n- L2: (2,2),(3,3)\n- L3: (2,3),(3,1)\n- L4: (2,4) (no column 3 point assigned yet)\n\nNow we need to cover column 4 points: (4,1),(4,2). Each line can intersect column 4 at most one point. So we need to assign these two points to two of the lines. Let's see which lines can pass through a column 4 point while staying collinear with their existing points.\n\nCheck L1: currently passes through (2,1) and (3,2). The line equation is y = x-1. At x=4, y=3. So L1 passes through (4,3), not (4,1) or (4,2). So L1 cannot cover any column 4 point.\n\nCheck L2: line y = x (through (2,2),(3,3)). At x=4, y=4, not (4,1) or (4,2). So L2 cannot cover column 4 points.\n\nCheck L3: line y = -2x+7 (through (2,3),(3,1)). At x=4, y = -8+7 = -1, not (4,1) or (4,2). So L3 cannot cover column 4 points.\n\nCheck L4: currently only has (2,4). We can choose a line through (2,4) and a column 4 point. Let's try (2,4) and (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. (2,4) and (4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed. So L4 can be line through (2,4) and (4,1). Equation: slope -1.5, y-4 = -1.5*(x-2) => y = -1.5x + 7. At x=5, y = -7.5+7 = -0.5, not needed. So L4 covers (2,4) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered (4,1). Still need to cover (4,2). None of L1-L3 can cover (4,2). L4 cannot because it's already determined by (2,4) and (4,1). Could we adjust assignments? Perhaps we could assign (4,2) to L1 or L2 or L3 by adjusting earlier assignments. Let's see if we can reassign column 3 points differently to allow a line to pass through (4,2). For each line, we have two points: one in column 2 and one in column 3. The line's slope is determined. We need to see if any of those lines can also pass through (4,2). Let's compute slopes for each possible pairing.\n\nWe have column 2 points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Column 3 points: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We need to assign each column 3 point to a distinct line (except one line will not get a column 3 point). Let's consider all possible assignments.\n\nGoal: find a line that passes through (2,b2) and (3,b3) and also (4,2). Solve for b2,b3 such that points are collinear.\n\nGeneral condition: three points (2,b2), (3,b3), (4,2) are collinear if the slope between (2,b2) and (3,b3) equals slope between (3,b3) and (4,2). That is (b3 - b2) / (3-2) = (2 - b3) / (4-3). So b3 - b2 = 2 - b3 => 2b3 = b2 + 2 => b3 = (b2 + 2)/2.\n\nSince b2 is integer from 1 to 4, b3 must be integer from 1 to 3. Let's test each b2:\n\n- b2=1 => b3 = (1+2)/2 = 1.5, not integer.\n- b2=2 => b3 = (2+2)/2 = 2, integer. So line through (2,2),(3,2),(4,2) is horizontal (slope 0), not allowed. So cannot use because slope 0 is not sunny.\n- b2=3 => b3 = (3+2)/2 = 2.5, not integer.\n- b2=4 => b3 = (4+2)/2 = 3, integer. So line through (2,4),(3,3),(4,2) has slope (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed (slope -1). So cannot.\n\nThus no line through a column 2 point and (4,2) can be sunny (except maybe slope 0 or -1). So (4,2) cannot be covered by any sunny line that also passes through a column 2 point. But we have to cover (4,2) with a sunny line, and each sunny line must pass through a column 2 point (since we need to cover all column 2 points). So (4,2) cannot be covered. Therefore k=4 is impossible for n=5.\n\nThus the impossibility for k≥4 may hold for all n≥3. Let's test n=4, k=4. For n=4, column 2 has points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Actually column 2 has n+1-2 = 3 points. So we have 4 sunny lines, but we only need to cover 3 points in column 2. So one sunny line will not intersect column 2 at any point (i.e., it could intersect column 2 at a non-integer point). But each sunny line can intersect column 2 at at most one point, but they don't have to intersect at integer points. So we could have a line that doesn't intersect column 2 at any integer point. So the previous argument for n=5 may not apply directly.\n\nBut we need to check if k=4 is possible for n=4. Let's try to find a construction.\n\nPoints for n=4:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) (4 points)\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) (3 points)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2) (2 points)\nColumn 4: (4,1) (1 point)\n\nWe have 4 lines total, k=4 sunny lines, no vertical lines. So we need to cover all points with 4 sunny lines. Each line can intersect each column at most one point. So each column i can have at most 4 points covered (since we have 4 lines). Column 1 has 4 points, so each line must intersect column 1 at exactly one point. So each line passes through a distinct point in column 1. So we have lines L1-L4 passing through (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) respectively.\n\nNow column 2 has 3 points, we need to assign each to a distinct line (since each line can intersect column 2 at most one point). So three of the lines will intersect column 2 at a point, one line will not intersect column 2 at an integer point. So we need to assign (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) to three lines.\n\nSimilarly column 3 has 2 points, assign to two lines. Column 4 has 1 point, assign to one line.\n\nThus we need to find lines passing through (1,a) and (2,b) for some a,b, and possibly also (3,c) and (4,d). Let's try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points.\n\nWe can treat this as a matching problem: assign each line a set of points (one per column) such that they are collinear.\n\nWe can try to find a solution manually.\n\nLet’s denote lines L_i for i=1..4, each passes through (1,i). We'll assign column 2 points to lines:\n\n- L1 (through (1,1)) could go to (2,2) maybe? Let's compute slope: (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L1: (1,1)-(2,2). Equation y = x. At x=3, y=3 (not in region). So L1 cannot cover any column 3 or 4 points.\n\n- L2 (through (1,2)) could go to (2,1) slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So cannot assign (2,1) to L2. Could assign (2,3) slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L2: (1,2)-(2,3). Equation: y-2 = 1*(x-1) => y = x+1. At x=3, y=4 (not in region). So L2 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\n- L3 (through (1,3)) could go to (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L3: (1,3)-(2,1). Equation: y-3 = -2*(x-1) => y = -2x+5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1 (not needed). So L3 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\n- L4 (through (1,4)) could go to (2,? ) maybe (2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L4: (1,4)-(2,2). Equation: y-4 = -2*(x-1) => y = -2x+6. At x=3, y = -6+6 = 0 (not needed). So L4 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\nThus with these assignments, none of the lines cover column 3 or 4 points. So we need to adjust assignments to allow some lines to cover column 3 and 4 points.\n\nWe need to assign column 3 points (3,1),(3,2) to some lines. Let's see which lines can pass through a column 3 point while also passing through a column 1 point and possibly a column 2 point.\n\nWe can consider lines determined by (1,a) and (3,c). Then we can see if they intersect column 2 at integer point.\n\nLine through (1,a) and (3,c) has slope (c-a)/(3-1) = (c-a)/2. At x=2, y = a + slope*(2-1) = a + (c-a)/2 = (a + c)/2. So for the line to intersect column 2 at integer point, (a + c) must be even. So we can assign column 2 point (2,b) where b = (a + c)/2, which must be integer and within {1,2,3}. So we need to find pairs (a,c) such that (a + c) is even and b = (a + c)/2 is in {1,2,3}.\n\nSimilarly, we need to assign column 4 point (4,d) maybe.\n\nLet's try to find a line that covers (1,1) and (3,2). Then a=1,c=2 => (a+c)/2 = 1.5, not integer, so line does not intersect column 2 at integer point. So that line would not cover any column 2 point, which is okay because we have 4 lines and only 3 column 2 points. So we could have one line that doesn't intersect column 2 at integer point. That line could cover (1,1) and (3,2). Let's compute slope: (2-1)/2 = 0.5, allowed. Equation: y-1 = 0.5*(x-1) => y = 0.5x + 0.5. At x=4, y = 2.5, not integer. So that line would not cover column 4 point either. So it would only cover (1,1) and (3,2). That's okay, but we still need to cover (4,1) and column 2 points.\n\nNow we need to cover column 2 points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Let's try to assign lines accordingly.\n\nWe have 4 lines: L1 passes through (1,1) and (3,2) (no column 2 point). L2 passes through (1,2) and (2,? ), L3 passes through (1,3) and (2,? ), L4 passes through (1,4) and (2,? ). Then we need to cover column 3 point (3,1) and column 4 point (4,1). Let's see if we can assign L2 to cover (2,1) and maybe (3,1) or (4,1). Let's try L2: (1,2)-(2,1) slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So L2 cannot go through (2,1). L2 could go through (2,2) slope = (2-2)/(2-1) = 0, not allowed. L2 could go through (2,3) slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L2: (1,2)-(2,3). Equation: y = x+1. At x=3, y=4 (not in region). So L2 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\nL3: (1,3) can go through (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L3: (1,3)-(2,1). Equation: y = -2x+5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1 (not needed). So L3 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\nL4: (1,4) can go through (2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L4: (1,4)-(2,2). Equation: y = -2x+6. At x=3, y = 0 (not needed). So L4 cannot cover column 3 or 4.\n\nThus this assignment fails to cover column 3 and 4 points.\n\nWe need to find a different assignment where some line passes through (1,a) and (3,c) and also (2,b) maybe, and also (4,d). Let's try to find a line that passes through (1,2) and (3,1). Then a=2,c=1 => (a+c)/2 = 1.5, not integer, so no column 2 integer point. So line L: (1,2)-(3,1) slope = (1-2)/2 = -0.5, allowed. At x=4, y = 2 + (-0.5)*(4-1) = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5, not integer. So that line covers (1,2) and (3,1) only.\n\nNow we need to cover column 2 points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Let's assign lines:\n\n- L1: (1,1)-(2,2) slope 1, allowed. At x=3, y=3 (not needed). So L1 covers (1,1),(2,2).\n\n- L2: (1,3)-(2,1) slope -2, allowed. At x=3, y = -2*3+5 = -1, not needed. So L2 covers (1,3),(2,1).\n\n- L3: (1,4)-(2,3) slope -1, not allowed. So cannot assign (2,3) to line through (1,4). Could assign (2,3) to line through (1,2) maybe? But (1,2) already used in L (covering (3,1)). But we could have L (1,2)-(3,1) not covering column 2. So we still need to cover (2,3). Could assign (2,3) to line through (1,1) maybe? But (1,1) already used in L1. Could assign (2,3) to line through (1,3) maybe? But (1,3) used in L2. Could assign (2,3) to line through (1,4) maybe? Let's try line through (1,4) and (2,3) slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So cannot. Could assign (2,3) to line through (1,2) but that line is already used for L (1,2)-(3,1). Could we modify L to also pass through (2,3)? Let's see if line through (1,2) and (3,1) also passes through (2,3). At x=2, line's y = 2 + (-0.5)*(2-1) = 2 - 0.5 = 1.5, not 3. So no.\n\nThus we cannot cover (2,3) with any line that passes through a column 1 point and is sunny, except maybe line through (1,1) and (2,3) slope = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. So we could assign (2,3) to line through (1,1). But (1,1) already used for L1 covering (2,2). But we could change L1 to cover (2,3) instead of (2,2). Let's try new assignment:\n\n- L1: (1,1)-(2,3) slope 2, allowed. At x=3, y = 2*3-1 = 5, not needed. So L1 covers (1,1),(2,3).\n\n- L2: (1,2)-(3,1) slope -0.5, covers (1,2),(3,1). No column 2 point.\n\n- L3: (1,3)-(2,1) slope -2, covers (1,3),(2,1).\n\n- L4: (1,4)-(2,2) slope -2, covers (1,4),(2,2).\n\nNow column 2 points are covered: (2,3) by L1, (2,1) by L3, (2,2) by L4. Good. Column 3 points: (3,1) covered by L2, (3,2) not covered. Column 4 point (4,1) not covered.\n\nWe need to cover (3,2) and (4,1). Could we adjust L2 to also cover (3,2) maybe? L2 currently passes through (1,2) and (3,1). The line equation: slope = (1-2)/2 = -0.5, y = -0.5x + 2.5? Actually compute: y-2 = -0.5*(x-1) => y = -0.5x + 2.5. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 2.5 = 1.0, which is (3,1). At x=4, y = -2 + 2.5 = 0.5, not integer. So L2 cannot cover (3,2) or (4,1). Could we change L2 to be line through (1,2) and (3,2)? That would be slope = (2-2)/2 = 0, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nMaybe we can assign (3,2) to L1? L1 passes through (1,1) and (2,3). Equation: slope = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, y = 2x -1. At x=3, y = 5, not 2. So no.\n\nAssign (3,2) to L3? L3 passes through (1,3) and (2,1). Equation: slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1, not 2.\n\nAssign (3,2) to L4? L4 passes through (1,4) and (2,2). Equation: slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, y = -2x + 6. At x=3, y = 0, not 2.\n\nThus (3,2) cannot be covered by any of these lines. So this assignment fails.\n\nThus we need a different assignment.\n\nLet's try to systematically search for a solution for n=4, k=4. This is a small combinatorial problem; we can try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points. Let's denote the lines by their slopes and intercepts. Since they cannot be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1, slopes can be any rational number not equal to 0, infinite, -1.\n\nWe need to cover points:\n\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\n(2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\n(3,1),(3,2)\n(4,1)\n\nWe need 4 lines, each line can intersect each column at most one point. So each line can have at most 4 points (one per column). Since we have 10 points, we need at least ceil(10/4) = 3 lines, but we have 4 lines, so it's plausible.\n\nWe need to assign each column's points to lines. Let's denote lines L1-L4. Each line must have at most one point per column. So we need to assign each point to a line such that no line gets two points from same column.\n\nWe can think of this as a 4-coloring of the points such that each column's points have distinct colors (except possibly some columns have fewer points than lines, so some colors may be unused). Additionally, each color class must be collinear (i.e., points assigned to same line must be collinear). Also each line must be sunny (slope not 0, -1, infinite). So we need to partition the points into at most 4 collinear sets with those constraints.\n\nWe can try to find a partition manually.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of slope 1 (diagonal) to cover some points. For slope 1 lines, points must satisfy y - x = constant. Let's compute y - x for each point:\n\n(1,1): 0\n(1,2): 1\n(1,3): 2\n(1,4): 3\n(2,1): -1\n(2,2): 0\n(2,3): 1\n(3,1): -2\n(3,2): -1\n(4,1): -3\n\nSo points with same y-x can be collinear with slope 1. For example, points with y-x = 0: (1,1),(2,2). That's a line of slope 1. Points with y-x = 1: (1,2),(2,3). That's a line of slope 1. Points with y-x = -1: (2,1),(3,2). That's a line of slope 1. Points with y-x = -2: (3,1) alone. Points with y-x = -3: (4,1) alone. Points with y-x = 2: (1,3) alone. Points with y-x = 3: (1,4) alone.\n\nThus we have lines of slope 1 covering pairs: (1,1)-(2,2), (1,2)-(2,3), (2,1)-(3,2). That's three lines. Then we have leftover points: (1,3),(1,4),(3,1),(4,1). That's four points left. We have one line left (since we need exactly 4 lines total). But we have 4 leftover points, each in distinct columns? Let's see: (1,3) column 1, (1,4) column 1, (3,1) column 3, (4,1) column 4. Actually (1,3) and (1,4) are both in column 1, so they cannot be on the same line because a line can intersect column 1 at only one point (since each line passes through exactly one point in column 1). So we cannot have a line covering both (1,3) and (1,4). So we need to assign them to different lines, but we have only one line left. So this partition fails.\n\nThus we need to use lines with other slopes to cover more points.\n\nConsider lines of slope 2: points satisfy y - 2x = constant. Compute y - 2x:\n\n(1,1): -1\n(1,2): 0\n(1,3): 1\n(1,4): 2\n(2,1): -3\n(2,2): -2\n(2,3): -1\n(3,1): -5\n(3,2): -4\n(4,1): -7\n\nPoints with same y-2x: -1: (1,1),(2,3). That's a line of slope 2. 0: (1,2) alone. 1: (1,3) alone. 2: (1,4) alone. -3: (2,1) alone. -2: (2,2) alone. -5: (3,1) alone. -4: (3,2) alone. -7: (4,1) alone.\n\nSo slope 2 line can cover (1,1) and (2,3). That's good.\n\nConsider slope 1/2: y - 0.5x = constant. Compute 2y - x:\n\n(1,1): 2-1=1\n(1,2): 4-1=3\n(1,3): 6-1=5\n(1,4): 8-1=7\n(2,1): 2-2=0\n(2,2): 4-2=2\n(2,3): 6-2=4\n(3,1): 2-3=-1\n(3,2): 4-3=1\n(4,1): 2-4=-2\n\nPoints with same 2y - x: 1: (1,1),(3,2). That's a line of slope 0.5. 3: (1,2) alone. 5: (1,3) alone. 7: (1,4) alone. 0: (2,1) alone. 2: (2,2) alone. 4: (2,3) alone. -1: (3,1) alone. -2: (4,1) alone.\n\nThus slope 0.5 line can cover (1,1) and (3,2). That's another line.\n\nConsider slope -2: y + 2x = constant. Compute y + 2x:\n\n(1,1): 1+2=3\n(1,2): 2+2=4\n(1,3): 3+2=5\n(1,4): 4+2=6\n(2,1): 1+4=5\n(2,2): 2+4=6\n(2,3): 3+4=7\n(3,1): 1+6=7\n(3,2): 2+6=8\n(4,1): 1+8=9\n\nPoints with same sum: 5: (1,3),(2,1). That's a line of slope -2. 6: (1,4),(2,2). That's a line of slope -2. 7: (2,3),(3,1). That's a line of slope -2. 8: (3,2) alone. 9: (4,1) alone. 3: (1,1) alone. 4: (1,2) alone.\n\nThus slope -2 lines can cover pairs: (1,3)-(2,1), (1,4)-(2,2), (2,3)-(3,1). That's three lines.\n\nNow we have many possible lines. Let's try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points.\n\nWe can try to use the three slope -2 lines covering (1,3)-(2,1), (1,4)-(2,2), (2,3)-(3,1). That's three lines. Then we need to cover remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(2,3?) Actually (2,3) is covered by line (2,3)-(3,1). So remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(2,2?) Actually (2,2) is covered by (1,4)-(2,2). So remaining: (1,1),(1,2),(2,3?) Already covered. (2,1) covered. (2,2) covered. (2,3) covered. (3,2) not covered. (4,1) not covered. So leftover points: (1,1),(1,2),(3,2),(4,1). That's 4 points. We have one line left (since we have used 3 lines). Can we find a line that covers these 4 points? They are in columns 1,1,3,4. A line can intersect column 1 at only one point, so cannot cover both (1,1) and (1,2). So we cannot cover all leftover points with a single line. So we need to adjust.\n\nMaybe we can use a different combination of lines.\n\nLet's try to use slope 1 lines for some pairs: (1,1)-(2,2), (1,2)-(2,3), (2,1)-(3,2). That's three lines. Then leftover points: (1,3),(1,4),(2,1?) Actually (2,1) is covered by (2,1)-(3,2). So leftover: (1,3),(1,4),(3,1),(4,1). That's four points. We have one line left. As before, cannot cover both (1,3) and (1,4) with same line. So not possible.\n\nMaybe we can use a line that covers three points: (1,1),(2,3),(3,5?) Not needed. Or (1,1),(3,2),(5,3?) Not needed. But we need to cover points across columns.\n\nLet's try to find a line that covers (1,1),(2,3),(3,5?) Not needed. So maybe we can cover (1,1),(2,3) with slope 2 line, and also (3,5) not needed. So that line covers two points.\n\nWe need to cover all points with 4 lines. Let's try to find a partition manually.\n\nWe can treat each line as a set of points with distinct columns. Since we have 4 columns, each line can have at most 4 points. So we need to partition 10 points into 4 sets, each set having at most one point per column. So the distribution of points per line could be (4,3,2,1) or (4,2,2,2) or (3,3,2,2) etc. Let's try to find a distribution that works.\n\nWe need to assign each column's points to lines. Column 1 has 4 points, so each line must have exactly one point from column 1 (since we have 4 lines). So each line gets one column 1 point. So each line will have at least one point (the column 1 point). So we have exactly one column 1 point per line.\n\nColumn 2 has 3 points, so three of the lines will have a column 2 point, one line will not have a column 2 point.\n\nColumn 3 has 2 points, so two lines will have a column 3 point, two lines will not.\n\nColumn 4 has 1 point, so one line will have a column 4 point, three lines will not.\n\nThus each line will have a pattern of which columns it covers. For each line, we can denote a binary vector (c1,c2,c3,c4) where c_i = 1 if line covers a point in column i. Since each line must have c1=1 (since each line must cover a column 1 point). Then we need to assign the other columns accordingly.\n\nWe need to assign column 2 points to three lines, column 3 points to two lines, column 4 point to one line. So we need to choose which lines get which columns.\n\nOne possible assignment: Let lines L1-L4 correspond to column 1 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Then assign column 2 points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) to L1,L2,L3 respectively (or any permutation). Then assign column 3 points (3,1),(3,2) to L1 and L2 (or any). Then assign column 4 point (4,1) to L1 (or any). So L1 would have points in columns 1,2,3,4 (i.e., (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1)). L2 would have (1,2),(2,2),(3,2). L3 would have (1,3),(2,3). L4 would have (1,4) only.\n\nNow we need to check if each line's assigned points are collinear and sunny.\n\nCheck L1: points (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1) are collinear horizontally (slope 0), which is not sunny (horizontal lines are not allowed). So L1 fails.\n\nThus we cannot assign L1 to have all points in column 1,2,3,4 because that would be horizontal. So we need to assign points such that each line is not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1.\n\nThus we need to avoid lines that have same y-coordinate across columns (horizontal) or same x-coordinate (vertical) or slope -1.\n\nThus we need to assign points such that each line's points have varying y-values across columns.\n\nLet's try to assign column 2 points differently: maybe assign (2,1) to L1, (2,2) to L2, (2,3) to L4 (skip L3). Then assign column 3 points: (3,1) to L2, (3,2) to L3. Then assign column 4 point (4,1) to L3. So we have:\n\n- L1: (1,1),(2,1)\n- L2: (1,2),(2,2),(3,1)\n- L3: (1,3),(3,2),(4,1)\n- L4: (1,4),(2,3)\n\nNow check each line:\n\nL1: points (1,1),(2,1) are horizontal (slope 0) -> not allowed.\n\nThus L1 fails.\n\nWe need to avoid any line having two points with same y-coordinate (horizontal). So we need to assign column 2 points such that each line's y-values differ across columns.\n\nThus we need to assign column 2 points to lines such that for each line, the y-coordinate at column 2 is different from the y-coordinate at column 1 (which is the column 1 point's y). Since column 1 points have y = 1,2,3,4 respectively. So for line L_i (i=1..4) passing through (1,i), we need to assign a column 2 point (2,b) where b ≠ i (to avoid horizontal). Also we need to avoid slope -1: slope = (b - i)/(2-1) = b - i. So slope = b - i. Slope -1 occurs when b - i = -1 => b = i - 1. So we must avoid b = i - 1. So for each i, we need to choose b ∈ {1,2,3} (since column 2 points are (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)) such that b ≠ i (to avoid horizontal) and b ≠ i - 1 (to avoid slope -1). Also b must be distinct across lines (since each column 2 point can be used only once). So we need a matching between i ∈ {1,2,3,4} and b ∈ {1,2,3} satisfying those constraints.\n\nLet's list possible b for each i:\n\n- i=1: column 1 point (1,1). b cannot be 1 (horizontal) and cannot be 0 (i-1=0) which is not in set. So b can be 2 or 3.\n\n- i=2: (1,2). b cannot be 2 (horizontal) and cannot be 1 (i-1=1) (slope -1). So b cannot be 1 or 2, leaving only b=3.\n\n- i=3: (1,3). b cannot be 3 (horizontal) and cannot be 2 (i-1=2) (slope -1). So b cannot be 2 or 3, leaving only b=1.\n\n- i=4: (1,4). b cannot be 4 (not in set) and cannot be 3 (i-1=3) (slope -1). So b cannot be 3, leaving b=1 or 2.\n\nThus we have constraints:\n\n- L1 (i=1) can take b=2 or 3.\n- L2 (i=2) must take b=3.\n- L3 (i=3) must take b=1.\n- L4 (i=4) can take b=1 or 2.\n\nBut we need each b used at most once. So we need to assign b=3 to L2 (forced). Then b=1 to L3 (forced). Then b=2 remains for L1 or L4. L1 can take b=2, L4 would then have no b left (since b=1 already used by L3, b=2 used by L1, b=3 used by L2). L4 would have no column 2 point, which is allowed (since we have only 3 column 2 points). So we can assign:\n\n- L1: (1,1) -> (2,2) (b=2)\n- L2: (1,2) -> (2,3) (b=3)\n- L3: (1,3) -> (2,1) (b=1)\n- L4: (1,4) -> no column 2 point.\n\nNow check slopes:\n\n- L1: slope = (2-1) = 1, allowed (slope 1).\n- L2: slope = (3-2) = 1, allowed.\n- L3: slope = (1-3) = -2, allowed.\n- L4: only (1,4) point, no slope yet.\n\nNow we need to assign column 3 points (3,1),(3,2) to lines. Each line can have at most one column 3 point. L4 currently has only (1,4). It could take a column 3 point. L1, L2, L3 also could take column 3 points, but we have only 2 column 3 points, so we need to assign them to two of the lines.\n\nWe need to ensure that adding a column 3 point to a line does not create a slope -1 or horizontal or vertical. Also the line must remain straight (i.e., the three points must be collinear). So we need to check if we can add a column 3 point to a line that already has two points (column 1 and column 2). For each line L_i with points (1,i) and (2,b_i), we can compute the line equation and see if it passes through any column 3 point (3,c). If it does, we can assign that point to that line. If not, we cannot assign that point to that line (unless we change the assignment of column 2 points). Let's compute the lines:\n\n- L1: passes through (1,1) and (2,2). Equation: slope = 1, line y = x. At x=3, y=3. So L1 passes through (3,3), which is not in region (since (3,3) has a+b=6 > n+1=5). So L1 does not pass through any column 3 point (which are (3,1) and (3,2)). So L1 cannot cover any column 3 point.\n\n- L2: passes through (1,2) and (2,3). Equation: slope = 1, line y = x+1. At x=3, y=4, not (3,1) or (3,2). So L2 cannot cover column 3 points.\n\n- L3: passes through (1,3) and (2,1). Slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. Equation: y - 3 = -2(x-1) => y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1, not (3,1) or (3,2). So L3 cannot cover column 3 points.\n\n- L4: currently only (1,4). It can be assigned a column 2 point? No, we already used all column 2 points. So L4 currently has only (1,4). We can assign a column 3 point to L4, and then maybe also a column 4 point. Let's see if we can assign both column 3 points to L4? No, L4 can have at most one column 3 point. So we need to assign one of the column 3 points to L4. The other column 3 point must be assigned to some line that currently has only (1,i) and (2,b_i) but we saw those lines cannot pass through column 3 points. So we need to adjust the assignment of column 2 points to allow some line to pass through a column 3 point.\n\nThus we need to reassign column 2 points differently, perhaps not using the forced assignments we derived earlier. Let's revisit the constraints for column 2 assignment. We derived constraints based on avoiding horizontal and slope -1. But we also need to consider that the line should be able to pass through a column 3 point later. So we need to choose assignments that allow lines to pass through column 3 points.\n\nLet's consider all possible assignments of column 2 points to lines L1-L4 (i=1..4) that satisfy constraints (no horizontal, no slope -1). Then for each assignment, compute the line equation and see if it passes through any column 3 point. Then we can assign column 3 points accordingly.\n\nWe have column 2 points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). We need to assign them to three of the four lines. Let's denote assignment as a mapping f: {L1,L2,L3,L4} -> {b ∈ {1,2,3}} ∪ {none}, with exactly three lines assigned a b, one line assigned none.\n\nConstraints for each line i (i=1..4) with assigned b:\n\n- b ≠ i (to avoid horizontal)\n- b ≠ i - 1 (to avoid slope -1)\n- b ∈ {1,2,3}\n- b must be distinct across lines.\n\nNow we need to consider all possible assignments.\n\nLet's list possible b for each i again:\n\ni=1: allowed b = {2,3}\ni=2: allowed b = {3} (since b ≠ 2, b ≠ 1)\ni=3: allowed b = {1} (since b ≠ 3, b ≠ 2)\ni=4: allowed b = {1,2} (since b ≠ 4 (not in set), b ≠ 3)\n\nThus the only possible assignments are:\n\n- L2 must get b=3.\n- L3 must get b=1.\n- L1 can get b=2 or 3, but b=3 already taken by L2, so L1 must get b=2.\n- L4 can get b=1 or 2, but b=1 already taken by L3, b=2 taken by L1, so L4 gets none.\n\nThus the assignment we found earlier is forced: L1: b=2, L2: b=3, L3: b=1, L4: none. So there is no alternative assignment. So the lines L1-L3 are forced as we had.\n\nThus we cannot change column 2 assignment to allow lines to pass through column 3 points. So we need to see if any of these lines can be extended to include a column 3 point. Let's compute the line equations again:\n\n- L1: (1,1)-(2,2) => y = x. At x=3, y=3 (not in region). So L1 cannot cover any column 3 point.\n\n- L2: (1,2)-(2,3) => y = x+1. At x=3, y=4 (not in region). So L2 cannot cover any column 3 point.\n\n- L3: (1,3)-(2,1) => y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -1 (not in region). So L3 cannot cover any column 3 point.\n\nThus none of the lines can cover column 3 points. So we cannot cover column 3 points with these lines. So we need to consider alternative approach: maybe we can assign column 3 points to L4 (which currently has only (1,4)). L4 can be assigned one column 3 point, say (3,1) or (3,2). Then we need to assign the other column 3 point to some line that currently has only (1,i) (i.e., maybe we can assign column 2 point to L4 instead of leaving it empty). But we saw assignment forced L4 to have none. However, maybe we can assign column 2 point to L4 and leave some other line without column 2 point. Let's see if we can assign column 2 points to L4 and leave some other line without column 2 point, while still satisfying constraints.\n\nOur earlier forced assignment assumed each line must have distinct b values, but we could assign column 2 points to L4 and leave L1 or L2 or L3 without column 2 point. However, we need to assign exactly three column 2 points to three lines. So we can choose any three of the four lines to assign column 2 points, as long as constraints hold. So we need to consider all subsets of three lines and assign b values accordingly.\n\nLet's enumerate possibilities:\n\nWe have lines L1 (i=1), L2 (i=2), L3 (i=3), L4 (i=4). We need to assign b values to three of them.\n\nConstraints for each line i:\n\n- Allowed b_i ∈ {1,2,3} \\ {i, i-1} (if i-1 ∈ {1,2,3}).\n\nList allowed b_i sets:\n\n- i=1: allowed {2,3}\n- i=2: allowed {3}\n- i=3: allowed {1}\n- i=4: allowed {1,2}\n\nThus L2 can only get b=3, L3 can only get b=1. So L2 and L3 must be among the three lines assigned column 2 points. L1 can get b=2 or 3, but b=3 already taken by L2, so L1 can only get b=2 if we assign L1. L4 can get b=1 or 2, but b=1 already taken by L3, b=2 maybe taken by L1 if L1 is assigned. So we have limited options.\n\nThus the only possible assignments of three lines are:\n\n- Assign L2 (b=3), L3 (b=1), L1 (b=2). L4 gets none. That's the assignment we had.\n\n- Assign L2 (b=3), L3 (b=1), L4 (b=2). L1 gets none. Let's check if L4 can get b=2: allowed for L4 is {1,2}, b=2 is allowed. So we can assign L4 b=2, leaving L1 without column 2 point. Let's see if that works.\n\nAssignment: L2: (1,2)-(2,3). L3: (1,3)-(2,1). L4: (1,4)-(2,2). L1: only (1,1). This satisfies constraints: L2 slope = 1, L3 slope = -2, L4 slope = (2-4) = -2, L1 only one point.\n\nNow we need to assign column 3 points (3,1),(3,2) to lines. L1 currently has only (1,1). It could be assigned a column 3 point. L2 line equation: passes through (1,2),(2,3) => y = x+1. At x=3, y=4, not (3,1) or (3,2). So L2 cannot cover column 3 points. L3 line equation: passes through (1,3),(2,1) => y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -1, not (3,1) or (3,2). So L3 cannot cover column 3 points. L4 line equation: passes through (1,4),(2,2) => slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, equation: y = -2x + 6. At x=3, y = 0, not (3,1) or (3,2). So L4 cannot cover column 3 points. L1 currently only has (1,1). We can assign a column 3 point to L1, but we need to ensure the line through (1,1) and that column 3 point is sunny (not horizontal, vertical, slope -1). Let's consider possibilities:\n\n- Assign (3,1) to L1: line through (1,1) and (3,1) is horizontal (slope 0), not allowed.\n\n- Assign (3,2) to L1: line through (1,1) and (3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2 = 0.5,